<p>1970</p>

<p>Joe's Bar of Mandan held a three-day "Polkafest" in October, beginning an annual tradition that continued for several years. Initially sponsored by Joe's Bar owners John and Vye Baron, the event was later coordinated by subsequent owner Bernie Stein and his family through 1985.</p>

<p>1975</p>

<p>Mandan annexed approximately 1,200 acres along Memorial Highway, also known as "The Strip." Located midway between Mandan and Bismarck, the area appealed to businesses seeking to serve customers on both sides of the river. Development had previously been limited because the land lay in a floodplain and experienced frequent spring flooding before the completion of Garrison Dam in 1956 and Crown Butte Dam in 1963.</p>

<p>The annexation was controversial. Much of the area lacked adequate drainage and sewer service, both of which would have to be upgraded to meet North Dakota Department of Health standards. Special assessments alone exceeded {% inflation 1000000, 1975 %}.</p>

<p>John Maher established a newspaper called <em>The Mandan News</em>, which published its first issue on September 30. Ken Rogers served as editor, and the newspaper was based at 303 First Street NE. Ken Maher later succeeded his father as publisher.</p>

